```

#### `IPlanGateService`

Defined in BRL. Code-level feature gating — checks whether a feature is available on the tenant's current plan. Reads `plan_id` from the JWT claim; no DB hit per request. The `PlanFeature` enum gates code paths; `PlanAttribute` drives what the marketing page displays — these are separate concerns.

```csharp
public interface IPlanGateService
{
    bool IsEnabled(PlanFeature feature);  // reads plan_id from ITenantContext claim
}

public enum PlanFeature
{
    MultiUserInvite,
    RoleManagement,
    TenantSettings,
    // future: AdvancedReporting, ApiAccess, etc.
}
```

#### Blazor UI gating

Features locked to a plan are wrapped in a `<PlanGate>` component (thin wrapper around `IPlanGateService`):

```razor
<PlanGate Feature="PlanFeature.MultiUserInvite">
    <Authorized>
        <InviteUserButton />
    </Authorized>
    <Upgrade>
        <UpgradePlanCallToAction />   <!-- shown to Solo users in place of the button -->
    </Upgrade>
</PlanGate>
```

Solo users see an "Upgrade to Team" prompt wherever team features would appear — they are never silently blocked without explanation.

#### Plan upgrade flow

**Phase 1–4 (current):** `SystemAdmin` flips `Tenant.PlanId` manually via the admin panel. No billing integration.

**Future phase:** A self-service upgrade page where the tenant owner can change their own plan. Billing integration (e.g., Stripe) connects here. The core feature-gate logic does not change — only the mechanism that updates `PlanId` changes.

#### Downgrade guard

If a `Team` tenant attempts to downgrade to `Solo` while more than one active user exists, `PlanService.DowngradeAsync` returns a `Result.Failure` with a message listing the blocking users. The tenant must deactivate or remove the extra users before the downgrade is allowed.

