Yes — right now `CmContext : DbContext` has no knowledge of Identity at all. To use ASP.NET Core Identity the context needs to inherit from `IdentityDbContext` and we need `ApplicationUser` defined first.

The minimum needed:

```csharp
// Domain/Entities/ApplicationUser.cs
public class ApplicationUser : IdentityUser<int>
{
    public Guid UserGuid { get; set; }
    public int? TenantId { get; set; }
    public string DisplayName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
```

Then the context signature changes to:

```csharp
public class CmContext : IdentityDbContext<ApplicationUser, IdentityRole<int>, int>
```

That gives you all the Identity tables (`AspNetUsers`, `AspNetRoles`, `AspNetUserRoles`, etc.) wired into the same `DbContext` and migration set. Using `IdentityRole<int>` keeps the role PK as `int` consistent with the rest of the schema — no need for a custom role class unless you want extra columns on roles later.

Want me to create `ApplicationUser` in Domain and update `CmContext` to inherit from `IdentityDbContext`?

