1. Primary Request and Intent:
   Building a cabinet-making Blazor Server app (.NET 10, EF Core 10, MySQL). This session focused on: wiring up configuration injection (web layer as composition root), EF migrations, full auth infrastructure using the .NET 10 pattern (SSR pages, code-behind files, IdentityCore + SignInManager), and MFA support with QR code enrollment and a per-session MFA reminder alert.

   Key user-stated principles enforced throughout:
   - Lower layers (DAL, BRL) must NEVER read appsettings or know about configuration classes — web layer passes typed DTOs down
   - All Razor pages use code-behind (.razor.cs) pattern — no @code blocks in .razor files
   - Action<TConfig> lambda pattern for DI extension methods
   - App.razor must NOT have @rendermode on `<Routes />` — HttpContext only available in statically-rendered components

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Blazor Server .NET 10 with SSR auth pages via `[ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]`
   - `HttpContext` only available as cascading parameter in statically-rendered (SSR) components — CRITICAL: setting @rendermode InteractiveServer on Routes breaks auth pages
   - `AddIdentityCore<User>` + `.AddRoles<IdentityRole<int>>()` + `.AddSignInManager()` + `.AddDefaultTokenProviders()` pattern (NOT AddIdentity)
   - Separate `AddAuthentication(options => {...}).AddIdentityCookies()` call
   - `[ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]` in `_Imports.razor` makes all account pages SSR
   - `IServiceScopeFactory` pattern for interactive components embedded in SSR context (avoids DbContext lifetime issues)
   - `RevalidatingServerAuthenticationStateProvider` for security stamp revalidation every 30 minutes
   - `AddCascadingAuthenticationState()` instead of manual `<CascadingAuthenticationState>` wrapper
   - TOTP QR code via QRCoder 1.4.3 — `PngByteQRCode.GetGraphic(5)` → base64 PNG in img tag
