content.text:
Yes, that makes perfect sense. The UoW acts as a repository factory scoped to the request — `GetRepository<TEntity>()` resolves a specific registered repo if one exists, otherwise falls back to `BaseRepository<TEntity>`. No more one-to-one repo-to-entity registrations for every table.

Here's the design:

```csharp
// IUnitOfWork in Domain/Interfaces/
IRepository<TEntity> GetRepository<TEntity>() where TEntity : CmEntity;

// UnitOfWork in DAL/
// - injects CmContext + IServiceProvider
// - caches resolved repos per type in a Dictionary<Type, object>
// - tries _sp.GetService<IRepository<TEntity>>() first (specific repo)
// - falls back to new BaseRepository<TEntity>(logger, _context)
```

Registration becomes:
```csharp
services.AddScoped<IUnitOfWork, UnitOfWork>();
// Only register specific repos when they have custom methods beyond base CRUD:
services.AddScoped<IRepository<CmUnit>, UnitRepository>();
// CmMaterial, CmMaterialDetail, etc. — no registration needed, UoW creates BaseRepository on demand
```

Services stop injecting individual `IRepository<T>` and inject `IUnitOfWork` instead:
```csharp
public MaterialService(IUnitOfWork uow, ...)
{
    _materials = uow.GetRepository<CmMaterial>();
    _details = uow.GetRepository<CmMaterialDetail>();
}
```

`RecordSuccessionService` also uses UoW internally — `GetEffectiveAsync<TDetail>()` and `SucceedAsync<TDetail>()` drop the repo parameter since the service calls `_uow.GetRepository<TDetail>()` itself.

Should I go ahead and implement all of this — `IUnitOfWork`, `UnitOfWork`, update `MeasurementService`, update the succession/material service interfaces, then build out the full material engine on top of it?

